echanism. The training procedure is summarized below. 1. Simulation We sample a large number of values of G 1 G_{1} from a uniform distribution. Then, for each G 1 G_{1} , the observable L 1 L_{1} is calculated using the Type-I seesaw mechanism. This process produces the training dataset 𝒟 \mathcal{D} : 𝒟 = { ( G 1 ( n ) , L 1 ( n ) ) } n = 1 N . \mathcal{D}=\left\{\left(G_{1}^{(n)},L_{1}^{(n)}ight)ight\}_{n=1}^{N}. (3.12) 2. Training Using the training data 𝒟 \mathcal{D} , we train the conditional vector field v t , L ​ ( G , θ ) v_{t,L}(G;	heta) . At each step, we sample t ∼ p ⁡ ( t ) t\sim p(t) and draw G t G_{t} from the conditional probability path defined in Eq. ( 3.7 ). Then, using the vector field in Eq. ( 3.11 ) as the teaching signal, we optimize θ 	heta to minimize the loss function given in Eq. ( 3.6 ). 3. Generation In this phase, the condition is fixed as the experimental value L exp L_{\mathrm{exp}} . First, an initial point G 0 ∼ 𝒩 ⁡ ( 0 , I ) G_{0}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,I) is sampled from the standard normal distribution. Next, we numerically solve the following ordinary differential equation, governed by the trained vector field v t , L exp ​ ( G , θ ) v_{t,L_{\mathrm{exp}}}(G;	heta) , from t = 0 t=0 to t = 1 t=1 . d ​ G t d ​ t = v t , L exp ​ ( G t , θ ) .  rac{dG_{t}}{dt}=v_{t,L_{\mathrm{exp}}}(G_{t};	heta). (3.13) Finally, we use the resulting G 1 G_{1} as a sample from the posterior distribution p ⁡ ( G | L exp ) p(G|L_{\mathrm{exp}}) . 4. Evaluation The set of observables calculated from the parameter G 1 G_{1} is defined as follows: T = { log 10 ⁡ ( Δ ​ m 21 2 / meV 2 ) , log 10 ⁡ ( Δ ​ m 31 2 / meV 2 ) , sin 2 ​ θ 12 , sin 2 ​ θ 13 , sin 2 ​ θ 23 } . T=\left\{\log_{10}\left(\Delta m_{21}^{2}/\mbox{meV}^{2}ight),\log_{10}\left(\Delta m_{31}^{2}/\mbox{meV}^{2}ight),\sin^{2}	heta_{12},\sin^{2}	heta_{13},\sin^{2}	heta_{23}ight\}. (3.14) Let T 1 T_{1} denote the value of T T calculated from G 1 G_{1} , T exp T_{\mathrm{exp}} represent the best-fit value of T T , and 3 ​ σ i 3\sigma_{i} denote the 3 ​ σ 3\sigma range for each component of T T . The accuracy of G 1 G_{1} is evaluated using the following χ 2 \chi^{2} value: χ 2 = 9 × ∑ i ( T 1 − T exp ) 2 ( 3 ​ σ i ) 2 . \chi^{2}=9	imes\sum_{i} rac{\left(T_{1}-T_{\mathrm{exp}}ight)^{2}}{\left(3\sigma_{i}ight)^{2}}. (3.15) A smaller χ 2 \chi^{2} value indicates higher accuracy of T 1 T_{1} . Because the forward map G ↦ L G\mapsto L is generally non-injective, conditioning on the experimental observables L exp L_{\exp} does not uniquely determine the underlying parameter point. Instead, the conditional distribution p ⁡ ( G | L exp ) p(G|L_{\exp}) may be multimodal and exhibit extended degeneracy directions. Accordingly, the objective of the generative model is not to identify a single best-fit solution, but to generate a representative ensemble of parameter points that captures the full structure of these degeneracies.

