inuous normalizing flow approach to conditional density estimation and is well suited to inverse problems with high-dimensional and multimodal posteriors. Indeed, Refs. [ 16 , 17 , 19 ] are cited as previous studies utilizing generative AI. Specifically, flow matching has already been employed in Ref. [ 19 ] as a conditional generative sampler to identify phenomenologically viable regions of neutrino-texture parameter spaces, thereby demonstrating its practical utility in flavor-physics analyses. In the present study, we extend this line of research by revisiting the underlying formulation of flow matching and more concretely demonstrating how data-driven inference can be used to explore and characterize nontrivial flavor structures, while also improving the efficiency of collecting informative data. 3.1 Method Let G G denote the parameters to be fitted and L L the calculated observables. Specifically, these are defined as follows: G = { Re Y i ​ α ν , Im Y i ​ α ν , M 1 M 3 , M 2 M 3 , log 10 ⁡ ( ⟨ H ⟩ 2 M 3 / meV ) , arg ⁡ M 1 , arg ⁡ M 2 } , L = { log 10 ⁡ ( Δ ​ m 21 2 / meV 2 ) , log 10 ⁡ ( Δ ​ m 31 2 / meV 2 ) , | ( U PMNS ) α ​ i | } . \displaystyle egin{split}G&=\left\{eal Y^{
