nisms and multilayer perceptrons (MLPs). In the position embedding , a position embedding p i p_{i} is added to each token to provide positional information for each component of the input vector. Since the self-attention mechanism cannot inherently distinguish the order of tokens without positional information, it is necessary to explicitly specify which token corresponds to which physical quantity. Through this process, for example, the model can differentiate components corresponding to Yukawa matrix elements from those corresponding to Majorana masses, thereby learning the dependencies between them. In the sinusoidal time embedding , the time t t is transformed into a 32-dimensional periodic embedding vector emb ⁡ ( t ) \mathrm{emb}(t) . Since the appropriate vector field varies depending on the position along the stochastic path, providing the network with information about t t enables it to represent transformations suitable for each stage of the transport process, from start to finish. In the global embedding MLP , a 100-dimensional conditional embedding c c is generated using the conditional vector L L and the time embedding emb ⁡ ( t ) \mathrm{emb}(t) . In the diffusion transformer (DiT) block , the conditional embedding c c is used to generate the shift, scale, and gate parameters for adaptive layer normalization. These parameters conditionally modulate the self-attention mechanism and the MLP, enabling the model to perform transformations that depend on the condition L L and the time step t t , while learning the dependencies among the components of G G . Finally, the output projection maps each token back to one dimension using Linear ⁡ ( 100 , 1 ) \mathrm{Linear}(100,1) , producing a vector field v t , L ​ ( G , θ ) v_{t,L}(G;	heta) with the same dimension as the input G G . Thus, the purpose of adopting the Transformer architecture is not merely to process high-dimensional inputs, but to capture the nonlinear dependencies and degenerate structures among the parameters that arise in the inverse problem of the Type-I seesaw mechanism, representing them as a vector field controlled by the observation condition L L and the time t t . In principle, the choice of a Transformer-based architecture is not unique; other network architectures can also be used to represent the conditional vector field. Nevertheless, in our numerical comparisons, the current Transformer architecture achieved higher generation accuracy than a simple fully connected network under otherwise comparable training settings. This improvement is attributed to the self-attention mechanism, which enables the network to model dependencies among the different components of G G , while incorporating the observation vector L L and the flow time t t through the conditional embedding. Therefore, we conclude that the Transformer architecture is a practical approach for improving the accuracy and stability of posterior estimation. G ∈ ℝ d G G\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{G}} Tokenization G i ↦ Linear ⁡ ( 1,100 ) G_{i}\mapsto\operatorname{Linear}(1,100) Position Embedding h i = Linear ⁡ ( G i ) + p i h_{i}=\operatorname{Linear}(G_{i})+p_{i} DiT Block × 5 	imes 5 hidden dim = 100 =100 heads = 10 =10 , MLP ratio = 4 =4 Linear ⁡ ( 100 , 1 ) \operatorname{Linear}(100,1) v t , L ​ ( G , θ ) ∈ ℝ d G v_{t,L}(G;	heta)\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{G}} t t Sinusoidal Time Embedding dim = 32 =32 max freq = 1000 =1000 L ∈ ℝ d L L\in\mathbb{R}^{d_{L}} Global Embedding MLP input: [ L , emb ⁡ ( t ) ] [L,\operatorname{emb}(t)] output: c ∈ ℝ 100 c\in\mathbb{R}^{100} AdaLN condition Figure 1: Transformer architecture for vector field estimation. 3.3 Numerical Implementation and Results This section explains the actual procedures for collecting an appropriate dataset, following the four stages described in Sec. 3.1 . All calculations are conducted with an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4070 Laptop GPU with 8 GB of VRAM. Simulation Each component of G G is sampled as follows. First, 10 7 10^{7} samples G i ~ ∼ 𝒰 ⁡ ( 